ACC-005 — Engineering Observation

Document ID: ACC-005
Title: Engineering Observation
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Observation, the process by which information about Engineering Reality is acquired for engineering cognition.

Engineering Observation serves as the interface between Engineering Reality and the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework. It captures facts, measurements, events, and conditions that may later be evaluated as engineering evidence.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all observations originating from Engineering Reality, regardless of their source or method of acquisition.

Engineering Observations may originate from:

Human engineers
Sensors and IoT devices
Engineering documents
CAD/BIM models
Simulations
Digital twins
Inspection reports
Images and videos
Laboratory testing
Monitoring systems
External engineering information sources
3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Observation is the acquisition of information describing Engineering Reality through observation, measurement, monitoring, inspection, simulation, documentation, or other verifiable means.

An observation records what is perceived or measured. It does not determine whether the information is valid, relevant, or sufficient.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Observation SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Reality-Based

Every observation SHALL originate from Engineering Reality.

4.2 Time-Bound

Every observation SHALL occur at a specific point or period in time.

4.3 Source-Identified

Every observation SHALL identify its originating source.

4.4 Contextual

Every observation SHALL include sufficient contextual information to support interpretation.

4.5 Verifiable

Observations SHOULD be capable of independent verification where practical.

4.6 Neutral

An observation SHALL record information without asserting engineering conclusions.

5. Relationships

Engineering Observation transforms Engineering Reality into observable information.

Engineering Reality
        │
        ▼
Engineering Observation
        │
        ▼
Engineering Evidence

Engineering Observation:

Originates from Engineering Reality.
Produces candidate information for Engineering Evidence.
Does not perform reasoning or interpretation.
6. Responsibilities

Engineering Observation is responsible for:

Capturing information about Engineering Reality.
Preserving the integrity of observed information.
Recording observation context.
Recording observation source.
Recording observation time.
Providing inputs for evidence evaluation.
7. Normative Requirements

Engineering Observations SHALL:

Originate from Engineering Reality.
Preserve observed information without modification.
Record source information.
Record temporal context.
Be distinguishable from engineering conclusions.
Be available for subsequent evidence evaluation.

Atlas SHALL NOT treat an observation as engineering evidence until it has been evaluated according to the Engineering Evidence specification.

8. Non-Goals

Engineering Observation does not:

Validate correctness.
Determine engineering significance.
Produce engineering conclusions.
Perform reasoning.
Create Engineering Understanding.
Create Engineering Knowledge.
9. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce additional observation mechanisms or metadata requirements without altering the canonical definition of Engineering Observation.